UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 24, Lesson 1: Dissolving', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Dissolving mixes a soluble material into a liquid so it seems to disappear; the material is still there as a solution. Insoluble materials stay visible.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPredict: sugar in warm water vs cold (same amounts); chalk dust in water.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible.\n\nWATCH FOR\nSaying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nTest five materials in water. Record soluble/insoluble and one rate note. Recover salt from saltwater by evaporation if allowed.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Dissolving mixes a soluble material into a liquid so it seems to disappear; the material is still there as a solution. Insoluble materials stay visible.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Test five materials in water. Record soluble/insoluble and one rate note. Recover salt from saltwater by evaporation if allowed. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Sugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible. Reasoning: I used dissolve by attending to solution rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Saying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates. Correction: Sugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible. Why: the correction restores accurate use of dissolve.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Saying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses dissolve. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about dissolve, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require a new example, named observations and a claim–evidence link. / Require misconception + corrected claim + evidence reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Sugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible. This matches the lesson skill for dissolve.","options":["Sugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible.","Saying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work."],"question":"Predict: sugar in warm water vs cold (same amounts); chalk dust in water. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Saying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Dissolving mixes a soluble material into a liquid so it seems to disappear; the material is still there as a solution. Insoluble materials stay visible.","Sugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible.","Careful use of solution with clear evidence.","Saying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising dissolve?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named solution, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the solution used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing measurement as if it were recorded."],"question":"A learner is practising dissolve. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence.","options":["Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation.","Saying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates.","Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone.","Decide from which object looks most exciting."],"question":"New examples: yeast making bread rise, a spinning fan, copper wire, a tadpole. Which habit makes a scientific sort or claim trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim.","options":["Saying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates.","Named observation plus how it supports the claim.","A longer story with no observation.","A label copied from the title with no evidence."],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about dissolve?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Sugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible. applies the science idea with evidence.","options":["Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Sugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible.","Saying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result."],"question":"Using this idea — Dissolving mixes a soluble material into a liquid so it seems to disappear; the material is still… — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable and control the rest.","options":["Stop early when you like the first result.","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer.","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same.","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster."],"question":"Which investigation habit keeps a comparison fair?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer needs a new object or context with evidence.","options":["Invent a dramatic result with no observation.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Test or describe a new object or context and cite fresh evidence.","Repeat the worked example objects unchanged."],"question":"Which next step best shows transfer of dissolve?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require a new example, named observations and a claim–evidence link.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses dissolve. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Sugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible. Reasoning: I used dissolve by attending to solution rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + corrected claim + evidence reason.","question":"Name one science misconception about dissolve, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Saying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates. Correction: Sugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible. Why: the correction restores accurate use of dissolve.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y4-w24-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise dissolve.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Material World","success_criteria":["I can use dissolve with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify solution and explain why it matters.","I can practise soluble independently on a new prompt.","I can notice insoluble needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Dissolving mixes a soluble material into a liquid so it seems to disappear; the material is still there as a solution. Insoluble materials stay visible. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Dissolving mixes a soluble material into a liquid so it seems to disappear; the material is still there as a solution. Insoluble materials stay visible."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about dissolve?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSalt in water dissolves (soluble). Sand in water settles (insoluble). Stirring and temperature can affect how fast something dissolves.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPredict: sugar in warm water vs cold (same amounts); chalk dust in water.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible.","explain":"Dissolving mixes a soluble material into a liquid so it seems to disappear; the material is still there as a solution. Insoluble materials stay visible.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Dissolving ideas.","caption":"Dissolved material still there.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Dissolve</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">soluble in liquid</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Insoluble</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">stays visible</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Salt in water dissolves (soluble). Sand in water settles (insoluble). Stirring and temperature can affect how fast somet Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Salt in water dissolves (soluble). Sand in water settles (insoluble). Stirring and temperature can affect how fast somet Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about dissolve?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSalt in water dissolves (soluble). Sand in water settles (insoluble). Stirring and temperature can affect how fast something dissolves. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Recovery evidence.","caption":"Five materials tested.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Salt recover</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">evaporate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">destroyed when dissolved</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about dissolve?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSaying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates. Here is a more productive model: Salt in water dissolves (soluble). Sand in water settles (insoluble). Stirring and temperature can affect how fast something dissolves.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSugar usually dissolves faster in warm; chalk remains mostly insoluble/visible.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Saying dissolved salt is “destroyed” is false — it can return when water evaporates. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about dissolve?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDissolving mixes a soluble material into a liquid so it seems to disappear; the material is still there as a solution. Insoluble materials stay visible. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 24, Lesson 1: Dissolving","unit_number":5,"unit_title":"Mixtures and changes","week":24,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nSalt in water dissolves (soluble). Sand in water settles (insoluble). Stirring and temperature can affect how fast something dissolves.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Dissolving ideas.","caption":"Dissolved material still there.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Dissolve</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">soluble in liquid</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Insoluble</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">stays visible</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Recovery evidence.","caption":"Five materials tested.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Salt recover</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">evaporate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">destroyed when dissolved</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-sci-y4-w24-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
